 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 2,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "THE NORFOLK BROADS. Rivers, reedbeds and about sixty shallow lakes called broads, with the same protection as a national park. East to St Benet's and the River Bure."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 15,
   "text": "WHERE THE BROADS CAME FROM. For centuries everyone thought the broads were natural lakes. In the 1950s the botanist Joyce Lambert showed they were medieval peat diggings: turves cut for fuel, millions of tonnes, which flooded as the sea rose.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_broads_peat",
   "title": "Medieval Peat Diggings"
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "HICKLING BROAD. The biggest of the Broads, and one of the best places in Britain for marsh harriers, bitterns and cranes. Waist-deep in most places. Do not test this.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_hickling",
   "title": "Hickling Broad"
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "THE SWALLOWTAIL. Britain's largest butterfly, yellow and black with little tails. Our own kind lives nowhere but the Broads, because its caterpillars eat only milk parsley, a fen plant.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_swallowtail",
   "title": "The Swallowtail"
  },
  {
   "x": 38,
   "y": 46,
   "text": "ST BENET'S ABBEY. A Saxon abbey that was never closed by Henry VIII: the Bishop of Norwich is still its abbot and comes by boat once a year to preach. In the 1700s a farmer built a windmill inside the ruined gatehouse.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_st_benets",
   "title": "St Benet's Abbey"
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "WHERRIES. Norfolk's cargo boats, with one huge black sail and a mast you could lower to slip under bridges. They carried coal, grain and reed along the rivers until the railways and lorries came.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_wherries",
   "title": "Norfolk Wherries"
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "DRAINAGE MILLS. Tall tarred brick towers with white sails, built to pump water off the marshes into the rivers. Dozens still stand along the Broads. Few still turn.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_windpumps",
   "title": "Drainage Mills"
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "THE BITTERN. A secret brown heron that hides in the reeds with its beak pointed at the sky. In spring the male booms like someone blowing over a bottle, and you can hear it miles away. In 1997 there were only eleven booming in Britain.",
   "plaque": "eastanglia_bittern",
   "title": "The Booming Bittern"
  }
 ],
